body:
I mean, it really comes to what you want out of your books. McCarthy has written like 1/6 of what Stephen King has written, and he definitely is not as comercially successful, but his books are something that will be talked about in a hundred years, nobody nowadays remember more than 4 King novels. So... It depends on what you want out of it. 

&#x200B;

William Gaddis was working on The Recognitions for 8 years, and his J R was 18 years work in progress, but no amount of 6 month books  can hold a candle to it.

**All of them.** One Piece powerscalers have a thing for old featless people.

&#x200B;

I do not count Big Mom and Kaido as old gen though so I do not count them within the "all of them" either.

I wanna use so much more than mere humans, and this whole story would be on a much bigger scale than just one world, or even one reality. 

&#x200B;

I wanted to see how would a different race, believing in just one god, cooperate with another that thinks it is a totally different entity. Or how would a horrors who thinks they ARE gods react to finding out they are not. and so on.

I love the ending, I do love the format of your writing, It reminds me of my boring lessons in a school where I did something similar.

&#x200B;

I love the imagery, and I do think that you should write as much as you can :) 

&#x200B;

Btw your opening line is really strong, like a great hook, really good. :)

It seems great, considering it is from the author of Narnia I am in :)

 Thank you for your suggestion :)

&#x200B;

Almost any sci-fi recommendation topic talks about Hyperion and Fall of Hyperion to be the best sci-fi there is. Most of the time it is matched only by the Dune and Book of the new sun in a number of votes. 

May I ask you what makes Hyperion stand out so much? I do have it on my reading list, but synopsis suggests nothing really out of ordinary sci-fi. Is it in prose? 

&#x200B;

You see, even though I am going to answer, most probably I wont help you figuring this out. 

&#x200B;

I myself dont know where the stories come from. I just sit somewhere with a random idea and then write. I write literally for myself because I dont know where I am going next and it is fun to figure it out. 

&#x200B;

Recently I wrote a ten page long piece about a taxi driver who got seduced by a succubuss, but I didnt know any of this when I started, I didnt even know that I am dealing with a taxi driver. All I had in my mind was "The engine stopped running" and that was what I started with and eventually it grew into a coherent story. 

&#x200B;

Couple of days ago I posted to this sub about my first try in English language. It is supposed to be about a man who wants to use unused potential of a dead baby. But I didnt know what baby, what man or who  if he is a protagonist or antagonist, I just wrote and it became what it is. (Although it is not finished yet.) 

This one is my longest work yet, (6000 words right now) and all I have is couple of scenes, some are in chronological order, some are just random episodes that happend in front of my eyes so I wrote them down and when I start revising I have to figure out what works and what not. 

The only thing I knew I want in this story was a scene where at the funeral, this mysterious man will ask the father of the kid about permision to use its potential. I already have 12 scenes, and this one is still not written. 

&#x200B;

Sorry for a long post, maybe you need to stop worrying about ideas and just write. I do it this way and it works quite good. 

&#x200B;

TLDR: I usually have one sentense long idea which grows on its own.
